'''''El Centre (Original, Sant Cristòfor)''''' is one of the original barris of [[Original (ciutat)|Original]], the historic centre of [[Sant Cristòfor]], [[Balboa]]. With a population of 33,181 across 2.72 km², it is one of the most densely populated barris in the ciutat, at 12,199 inhabitants per square kilometre. El Centre grew around a hill set slightly inland from the bay shore, developing from its early colonial origins into a central hub of markets, homes, and commercial life. The barri retains a significant stock of preserved colonial buildings that survived the [[Great Adonian War]] despite heavy bombardment, giving it a distinctive architectural character amid the surrounding post-war reconstruction.

El Centre was historically the centre of financial activity in Sant Cristòfor, home to the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] — the national stock exchange — and the [[Bank of Balboa]] headquarters. Most financial activity, however, was concentrated in the western, waterfront portion of the barri, which was carved off in the late 1980s to form the [[Districte Financer (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Financer]]. The remaining inland portion of El Centre retains the Bolsa and the Bank of Balboa but has taken on a more residential and mixed-use character, with a high cost of living and a concentration of vacation rentals and pied-à-terre apartments. El Centre is part of [[Districte IV (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte IV]], together with the neighbouring barris of [[La Vila Nova (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|La Vila Nova]], [[Molls Superiors (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Molls Superiors]], and [[La Garriga (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|La Garriga]].

== Geography ==

El Centre occupies a central position within Original, built around a low hill set slightly inland from the [[Badia de Sant Cristòfor]]. The [[Districte Financer (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Financer]] lies immediately to the west, between El Centre and the bay shore. The barri is bounded by the [[Districte Financer (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Financer]] and [[Plaça Major (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Plaça Major]] to the west, [[Barri del Te (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Barri del Te]] to the north, and [[La Garriga (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|La Garriga]] to the south and east. The [[Gran Avinguda]], the main east–west spine of the ciutat, crosses through the barri's interior. The diagonal [[Avinguda Llibertat]] traverses El Centre from the [[Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal|Central Terminal]] southeast toward [[Sant Jordi]], serving as one of the principal boulevards laid out during the post-war reconstruction.

== History ==

[[File:El Centre colonial streetscape.png|thumb|right|300px|Preserved colonial streetscape in El Centre, with converted mercantile houses lining a narrow street around the hill]]

=== Origins and colonial growth ===

El Centre was one of the original neighbourhoods of Sant Cristòfor, established during the colonial period around a low hill a short distance inland from the bay shore. The hill provided a defensible position slightly above the waterfront, and the settlement grew quickly as a central hub, with markets, homes, and mercantile establishments clustering around it. The barri developed in parallel with the waterfront commercial district to its west, which served the [[Camí Ral]] trade corridor between the [[Shedan Ocean|Shedan]] and [[Aethiopian Ocean]] coasts.

The 1866 racial districting ordinance formalised the city's ethnic geography, assigning the mixed commercial class to the waterfront. El Centre became the district of the mercantile and professional population — Volisanian, Zong, and mixed-race traders, bankers, and brokers — while its inland position around the hill also attracted residential development for the city's growing middle class.

=== Shining Star era (1899–1950) ===

{{main|Shining Star era}}

During the Shining Star era, El Centre became the centre of financial activity in Sant Cristòfor. The [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] was established in 1903, with its trading floor housed in purpose-built premises at 39 Carretera de Pedro González from 1922. The exchange anchored a financial services corridor — brokerages, law offices, insurance firms, and investment houses clustering within walking distance of the trading floor. The [[Sant Cristòfor Tramway Company]] operated streetcar lines connecting the commercial district to the [[Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal|Central Terminal]] and the wider city.

The first wave of skyscraper construction in the area began in the 1910s, as Zong trading companies, banks, and insurance firms built increasingly tall headquarters. The most prominent was the [[Torre del Comerç]] (Commerce Tower), a 24-storey Zong bank headquarters completed in 1931, which became the tallest building in Balboa at the time. These towers were concentrated in the western, waterfront portion of El Centre, which would later become the Districte Financer.

=== Great Adonian War ===

{{main|Great Adonian War}}

During the [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1947), El Centre was subjected to heavy bombardment during the prolonged siege of Sant Cristòfor. The western portion of the barri, with its concentration of financial institutions and commercial towers, was a primary target. Despite the intensity of the bombardment, a significant number of colonial-era buildings in the inland portion of El Centre survived the war. Their masonry construction and position on slightly higher ground around the hill spared them from the complete destruction that devastated the wooden tenements of the southern barris. The Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor building survived with structural damage and was restored in the post-war period.

=== Post-war reconstruction ===

[[File:El Centre revival architecture.png|thumb|left|300px|Post-war Volisanian traditional revival buildings in El Centre, designed to mimic the colonial style with masonry façades, shuttered windows, and tiled roofs]]

The post-war reconstruction under the [[Adonian Community]] mandate preserved much of El Centre's surviving colonial fabric while modernising the barri's infrastructure. The boulevard system — wide, tree-lined avenues laid out to replace the narrow, organic streets destroyed during the war — reshaped the barri's street pattern where damage was heaviest. The [[Gran Avinguda]] and the diagonal [[Avinguda Llibertat]] were laid out during this period, with streetcar tracks embedded in the boulevards as part of the rebuild. In areas where colonial buildings survived, the original street pattern was retained. Where buildings had been destroyed, the reconstruction employed a Volisanian traditional revival style, with new structures designed to mimic the colonial architecture — replicating the masonry façades, shuttered windows, and tiled roofs of the original buildings. This deliberate architectural continuity gives El Centre a distinctive, cohesive character that blends genuinely preserved colonial buildings with post-war replicas, a combination often difficult to distinguish at a glance.

=== Formation of the Districte Financer ===

In the late 1980s, the rapid construction of modern high-rise office towers along the waterfront portion of El Centre prompted the municipal government to establish the [[Districte Financer (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Financer]] as a separate barri. The new district was carved from the western, bay shore section of El Centre, absorbing the Torre del Comerç and the densest concentration of post-war and modern commercial towers. The split formalised a functional division that had been growing for decades: the waterfront had become the centre of high-rise financial office space, while the inland portion around the hill retained the Bolsa, older commercial buildings, and a growing residential population.

=== Modern development ===

[[File:Torre del Comerciant.png|thumb|right|300px|The Torre del Comerciant rising above the low-rise colonial fabric of El Centre]]

From the 2000s onward, El Centre experienced a new wave of office and residential development, driven by demand from the financial services sector and the growing corporate presence along the [[Balboa Canal]] corridor. The [[Torre del Comerciant]], a 48-storey office tower completed in 2002 at 60 Carretera de Pedro González, was developed by [[BdSC Corporació]] — the parent company of the Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor — and stands one block from the stock exchange building. At 222 metres, it is among the tallest buildings in Sant Cristòfor.

In the 2010s, [[Grup Ciutat Nova]] developed additional mixed-use projects in El Centre, combining retail, residential, and office space. The barri's high cost of living and central location have made it a hub of vacation rentals and pied-à-terre apartments, with investors and wealthy professionals maintaining second homes in the preserved colonial buildings and newer developments alike.

The [[Bank of Balboa]], the central bank of Balboa, established its headquarters at 1400 Avinguda Llibertat in El Centre, placing the institution within proximity to the Bolsa, the Districte Financer, and the [[National Assembly of Balboa|National Assembly]].

=== Redevelopment and preservation ===

The pace of redevelopment in El Centre has created a visible divide within the barri. The western portion, closest to the [[Districte Financer (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Financer]], is being rapidly redeveloped with modern mixed-use projects that replace older commercial buildings and post-war revival structures. Developers, drawn by the proximity to the financial district and the canal corridor, have acquired properties along the western edge of the barri for office and luxury residential construction, often at prices that have displaced long-standing commercial tenants and small businesses.

In contrast, the area around the hill and the southern portion of El Centre retain the classic character that has defined the barri since its colonial origins. The preserved colonial buildings and post-war Volisanian traditional revival architecture that dominate these streets remain largely intact, with converted homes, small shops, and low-rise residential properties maintaining the dense, historic streetscape. Property owners in these areas have been more resistant to redevelopment, and the narrower streets and protected status of many colonial-era structures have limited the scale of new construction.

Local opposition to the western redevelopment has grown since the late 2010s, with residents and preservation advocates arguing that the demolition of post-war revival buildings erodes the barri's architectural cohesion and that the influx of luxury developments is pricing out the remaining commercial tenants who serve the residential community. Community organisations have called for stricter height limits and design review for new construction in the western portion, citing the contrast with the protected colonial core around the hill as evidence that the barri's character is vulnerable to incremental erosion. Proponents of redevelopment counter that the western edge of El Centre has always been the most commercially oriented section and that new construction is a natural continuation of its role as the barri's business front.

== Landmarks and institutions ==

El Centre contains several principal financial and civic institutions of Sant Cristòfor:

