The [[Torre del Comerç]] (Commerce Tower), completed in 1931 as the headquarters of a Zong bank, was the tallest building in Balboa during the Shining Star era. Its reinforced concrete frame survived the Great Adonian War. Following the formation of the Districte Financer in the late 1980s, the Torre del Comerç now falls within the boundaries of the Districte Financer.

The barri's preserved colonial buildings — surviving from the original settlement around the hill — constitute a significant architectural heritage. These structures, which withstood the bombardment of the Great Adonian War, include colonial-era mercantile houses, market buildings, and residential blocks that predate the Shining Star skyscrapers. Many of these buildings have been converted from their original commercial or mercantile use into residential properties, housing the vacation rentals and pied-à-terre apartments that characterise the barri's modern residential market. Their survival gives El Centre a denser, more varied streetscape than the surrounding barris that were largely rebuilt after the war, while the post-war Volisanian traditional revival buildings fill the gaps with structures designed to match the colonial style.

== Transport ==

El Centre is one of three barris in Original — together with [[La Sagrada (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|La Sagrada]] and [[Les Vil·les (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Les Vil·les]] — that have no [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|metro]] station. The barri is served by the streetcar and bus networks.

The [[T3 streetcar line]] runs along Avinguda Llibertat, connecting the [[Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal|Central Terminal]] to [[Sant Jordi]] via El Centre. The streetcar tracks are embedded in the boulevard's surface, part of the post-war reconstruction design that integrated public transport with the widened avenues.

The bus network provides feeder services within the neighbourhood blocks, carrying passengers to the nearest metro station or boulevard stop. Bus routes serve the narrow streets and residential areas of El Centre that are too far from a metro entrance or a streetcar stop. The bus service operates 24 hours a day, with night service taking over the streetcar routes along the boulevards when the streetcar closes.

== Economy ==

[[File:El Centre Avinguda Llibertat.png|thumb|right|300px|Avinguda Llibertat, the diagonal boulevard laid out during post-war reconstruction, with streetcar tracks embedded in the road surface]]

El Centre historically functioned as the centre of financial activity in Sant Cristòfor. While the bulk of financial office space has shifted to the adjacent [[Districte Financer (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Financer]] to the west, the barri retains the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] and the [[Bank of Balboa]], along with a concentration of law offices, accounting firms, and professional services tied to the stock exchange. The [[Torre del Comerciant]] and surrounding office buildings house financial services firms and investment managers.

The barri's residential character has intensified in recent decades, with preserved colonial buildings — many converted from their original mercantile use into residential properties — and central location attracting a wealthy population. The high cost of living and the concentration of vacation rentals and pied-à-terre apartments have made El Centre one of the most sought-after residential areas in Original. The barri's economy is closely linked to the [[Balboa Canal]] corridor, with logistics, insurance, and trade-related firms maintaining offices in the district, and the proximity to the [[International City of Areza]] and the [[Adonian Community]] institutions generating additional demand for professional services.

